WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.667
Hello, hello! How are you?

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.000
What a pleasure
to greet you all!

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.634
Hi, everyone.
My name is Esteban Macías

00:00:06.634 --> 00:00:10.100
and I'm here
in my beautiful Mexico City,

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.167
the great Tenochtitlan.
And here we are.

00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:15.167
I'm so excited to be
joined by some of the stars

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:16.334
and the filmmakers

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:18.767
of Marvel Studios'
Black Panther:

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:20.334
Wakanda Forever.

00:00:20.334 --> 00:00:22.067
Wakanda Forever.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.434
I love how that sounds.
It sounds fantastic.

00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:27.000
We're going to be talking
all about the new movie,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.667
including a few questions
submitted by you, the fans,

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:32.000
but first, please let me
give you a warm welcome

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.534
and introductions to the half...
To the folks joining us today.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:38.834
So, first of all,
Mr. Director, Ryan Coogler.

00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:41.234
Mr. Producer, Nate Moore.

00:00:41.234 --> 00:00:43.634
And what a front row we have.

00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:45.133
What a front row.

00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:48.434
Letitia Wright,
Shuri in the movie.

00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:50.934
We have Nakia, Lupita Nyong'o,

00:00:50.934 --> 00:00:52.667
which I know

00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:54.901
understands everything
I'm saying in Spanish

00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:56.300
and I love that.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.767
And of course, Mabel Cadena,

00:00:57.767 --> 00:01:01.267
which is Namora,
the new character in this movie.

00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:02.868
-Welcome, Mabel...
-Thank you.

00:01:02.868 --> 00:01:05.400
...to our country,
to Mexico City.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.868
And unfortunately
I gotta tell you that Tenoch,

00:01:07.868 --> 00:01:10.200
Tenoch Huerta,
couldn't join us today,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.234
but we know he's proud
and excited for everyone

00:01:13.234 --> 00:01:16.300
to come watch
this cast November 11th.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.100
Tenoch couldn't be
with us today.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.434
He sent us a little message,
but he expects us all

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:23.400
to see the film
this November 11th.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.300
He's very excited
about this cast,

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.934
and well,
how about we get started?

00:01:27.934 --> 00:01:31.901
We welcome you to Mexico City.
Welcome to Mexico City.

00:01:31.901 --> 00:01:34.667
And I have to tell you,
guys, I'm a huge Marvel fan,

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:35.968
I love the MCU.

00:01:35.968 --> 00:01:39.367
Of course, this new movie
I think it's great.

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:43.100
I got the chance to see it
yesterday and I loved it.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.501
And what about
we start with you, Ryan?

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:47.968
First of all,
I want to ask you, of course,

00:01:47.968 --> 00:01:49.968
Ryan, Nate, and for everyone.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.501
The first one
was a huge spectacle,

00:01:53.501 --> 00:01:55.167
but I don't know how you do it.

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:57.267
This one looks bigger.

00:01:57.267 --> 00:02:00.234
Action scenes, new characters,

00:02:00.234 --> 00:02:02.601
a lot of new locations,

00:02:02.601 --> 00:02:06.067
so tell me how it was
to return to Wakanda,

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:09.834
but also to bring
to this new movie

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:13.033
the huge
and lovely city of Talokán.

00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:14.100
How was it?

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.634
It was, it was fantastic

00:02:16.968 --> 00:02:20.367
to have the opportunity to work
with these actors again,

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:22.868
to have a chance to deepen

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:25.200
our relationship
with the characters,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.067
see new parts of Wakanda,

00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:28.634
and it was...

00:02:28.634 --> 00:02:31.133
It was truly amazing

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:34.100
to be able to bring
Talokán to the big screen.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.167
Especially the characters,
Namor, Namora, Attuma.

00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:39.434
It was fantastic for me

00:02:39.434 --> 00:02:42.100
and I think

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.067
I was not in the minority
of folks involved

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.501
with the film
who are the best swimmers.

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:50.934
I had to commit to learn
how to swim so I could be there

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:53.467
with the actors
and with the camera team

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:54.901
and the underwater team.

00:02:54.901 --> 00:02:57.133
I think Nate kind of
figured it out before.

00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:00.167
But it was, it was

00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:01.834
just an amazing opportunity.

00:03:01.834 --> 00:03:05.167
And also to learn
more about the cultures

00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:07.267
of Mesoamerica,

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:09.834
the pre-Columbian
history of Mesoamerica

00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:12.467
and to make

00:03:12.467 --> 00:03:14.267
friends with new talented folks

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:16.367
from different parts
of the world like Mabel,

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:18.033
Tenoch, and Alex.

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:20.901
It was truly,
it was truly a fantastic

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:23.634
opportunity and a blessing.

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:25.334
[Esteban]
For you, Nate, how was,

00:03:25.334 --> 00:03:27.033
-how was filming?
-Yeah,

00:03:27.033 --> 00:03:28.701
it was really exciting.
I mean, you know.

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:31.067
When Ryan brought to us
what he thought

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:33.033
the second movie could be,

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:34.934
it was impressive and daunting

00:03:34.934 --> 00:03:37.067
because whenever you
think about creating,

00:03:37.067 --> 00:03:39.601
not only revisiting Wakanda,
but creating this new world

00:03:39.601 --> 00:03:41.234
of Talokán that is underwater,

00:03:41.234 --> 00:03:43.968
you realize there's a whole host
of other logistical questions

00:03:43.968 --> 00:03:45.567
you're going to have to answer.

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.300
And Ryan is such
a grounded filmmaker

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.200
and wanted everything
to feel tactile

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.434
and wanted performers
to be in the water,

00:03:51.434 --> 00:03:53.567
to really move as if they were

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:56.667
in submerged environments
which just created

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:59.734
an interesting extra wrinkle
past mid the movie

00:03:59.734 --> 00:04:01.634
of all these water tanks
and training people

00:04:01.634 --> 00:04:03.901
to be comfortable
performing in water,

00:04:03.901 --> 00:04:06.801
but I think all that
fidelity shows up on screen.

00:04:06.801 --> 00:04:09.200
And so, when you watch
the movie, hopefully,

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.767
audiences are really
immersed in this world

00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:14.367
because the actors
and actresses are really there.

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:17.734
Were you expecting that,
Lupita, Letitia?

00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:21.133
The brand new worlds
coming to this movie?

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:23.801
Not at all.

00:04:23.801 --> 00:04:26.767
I mean, when Ryan told me
what the idea was,

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.701
I was kind of shocked
and confused.

00:04:32.701 --> 00:04:34.133
I mean,
when you read the script,

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:35.534
you're just like "Well,
how are we going to make

00:04:35.534 --> 00:04:37.400
half this movie underwater?"

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.234
You know? It just seems like a
very impossible task

00:04:40.234 --> 00:04:43.467
to do, but then I remembered
also how much

00:04:43.467 --> 00:04:45.000
Ryan likes water

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.634
'cause in the first
film we had water.

00:04:47.634 --> 00:04:49.434
-A lot of water.
-We had water.

00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:51.901
He keeps... And then
it was water to our shins,

00:04:51.901 --> 00:04:55.801
and then decided to just
submerge us entirely.

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:58.701
But I was also
very excited to have that

00:04:58.701 --> 00:05:03.868
opportunity to learn
how to befriend the water,

00:05:03.868 --> 00:05:06.300
how to be comfortable
in the water

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.434
because I think
that's a very important skill

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:11.033
and one that I think
too often we overlook.

00:05:12.234 --> 00:05:14.701
Indeed. I want
to ask you, Mabel.

00:05:14.701 --> 00:05:18.400
I'm going to ask this in Spanish
because we're in Mexico City.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.400
We're in Mexico.
Well, what was it like

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:24.200
to join this group
and play this character?

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.501
It was amazing, really,
because I never expected

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:30.801
to have an opportunity
like this. And I worked

00:05:30.801 --> 00:05:33.267
so hard to be proud
of the career

00:05:33.267 --> 00:05:36.534
that I have today in this city,
in this country,

00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:41.200
and receiving this opportunity
helped me remind myself

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.367
that I had to work and develop
new skills, you know?

00:05:44.367 --> 00:05:48.300
That I could make new paths
possible for myself.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.801
I'm so grateful to Nate,
to Ryan,

00:05:51.801 --> 00:05:53.534
to this whole team

00:05:53.534 --> 00:05:56.367
because for me
it was a "Hey, Mabel.

00:05:56.367 --> 00:05:59.767
Remember that you can
change the pace and the story

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:02.133
of your own life," you know?
"Remind yourself.

00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:05.234
Remember your power,
remember your ability

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:08.067
to create new worlds," you know?

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.667
So it opened up
a whole universe for me

00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:13.734
that was maybe
impossible before.

00:06:13.734 --> 00:06:16.334
It was like, "No,
I'm never going to be there."

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:18.667
It was like "I have
to deal with a language

00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:21.334
that's not mine, I have to deal
with a lot of things

00:06:21.334 --> 00:06:23.634
that seem
a little bit impossible"

00:06:23.634 --> 00:06:26.801
and this was like
"Oh, my God, I'm here!

00:06:26.801 --> 00:06:29.801
With this team,
with this amazing director

00:06:29.801 --> 00:06:33.334
and with this amazing producer,
and they give me all the tools

00:06:33.334 --> 00:06:36.300
for made this story

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.267
real for me."

00:06:38.267 --> 00:06:40.267
So I'm very grateful for that.

00:06:40.267 --> 00:06:43.067
And it's fantastic to see
a Mexican such as yourself

00:06:43.067 --> 00:06:46.434
in a movie like this one.
It's so great.

00:06:46.434 --> 00:06:51.300
Ryan and Nate, how was to...

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.901
Or the approach
to bring back Wakanda

00:06:54.901 --> 00:06:56.834
and the world of Wakanda,

00:06:56.834 --> 00:06:59.267
but at the same time
honoring the memory

00:06:59.267 --> 00:07:00.634
of Chadwick Boseman

00:07:00.634 --> 00:07:03.567
and respecting his huge legacy?

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:07.367
Yeah, I mean...

00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:12.601
I can say none of us obviously
expected to lose Chad and so...

00:07:13.801 --> 00:07:15.601
You know, there's
a version of this movie

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:17.367
where his character
was a big part of it,

00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:20.100
but once we all felt
that loss we were able

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:23.133
to kind of process
what it meant,

00:07:23.133 --> 00:07:25.100
we had to figure out
how to recenter the movie

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.834
in a way that both
respected us as artists

00:07:27.834 --> 00:07:30.767
and didn't force us into places
we were uncomfortable with,

00:07:30.767 --> 00:07:33.434
but also gave audiences
the opportunity to revisit

00:07:33.434 --> 00:07:35.868
a world they loved, you know?
I think

00:07:35.868 --> 00:07:38.667
I would go as far... Like,
Chad understood what Wakanda

00:07:38.667 --> 00:07:40.868
meant to people,
so we had to figure out

00:07:40.868 --> 00:07:42.667
how to revisit that place,

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:46.000
but also infuse
into the storytelling

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.434
the very real loss
we were feeling,

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:50.133
the loss that I think
audiences were feeling too.

00:07:50.133 --> 00:07:52.901
And so we tried to tell
a story that was honest

00:07:52.901 --> 00:07:56.367
to those emotions
and that allowed the characters

00:07:56.367 --> 00:07:58.400
to grieve the loss of T'Challa

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.534
in the same way we were
grieving the loss of the man.

00:08:00.534 --> 00:08:02.200
And so, giving them the choice,

00:08:03.334 --> 00:08:06.701
the question became
how do we best tackle this,

00:08:06.701 --> 00:08:08.968
again, in a way
that expanded the legacy

00:08:08.968 --> 00:08:10.100
of the man in the world,

00:08:11.601 --> 00:08:14.901
but allowed us to deliver
something we could believe in.

00:08:14.901 --> 00:08:19.868
Letitia, you were so close
to him in the first movie,

00:08:19.868 --> 00:08:23.400
so in that regard, how was

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:25.901
coming back to this world?

00:08:28.567 --> 00:08:32.234
So, yeah. The first conversation
was discussing with...

00:08:32.234 --> 00:08:35.200
With my director
how we were going to...

00:08:36.267 --> 00:08:38.834
We were going to come together
as a team to honor my bro

00:08:38.834 --> 00:08:43.167
and at first it was
really weird for me

00:08:43.167 --> 00:08:45.000
'cause I was still...

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.133
I was just really angry
at the world.

00:08:47.133 --> 00:08:49.834
I was just really upset
and I just didn't...

00:08:49.834 --> 00:08:52.534
I just didn't want to do it
without him.

00:08:52.534 --> 00:08:54.634
And I just had to...

00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:57.167
I just really had to trust
my director.

00:08:57.167 --> 00:09:00.467
I had to trust him
as our leader, as our brother.

00:09:00.467 --> 00:09:02.968
Someone who is
incredibly sensitive

00:09:02.968 --> 00:09:05.901
and instinctive as well.

00:09:05.901 --> 00:09:08.467
He's really instinctive
about his choices.

00:09:08.467 --> 00:09:10.801
He doesn't just
make it just to make it,

00:09:10.801 --> 00:09:13.000
so I knew when he was saying

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.267
"There's a light here
that we could tap into..."

00:09:17.501 --> 00:09:20.501
That changed my perspective
and I saw the opportunity

00:09:20.501 --> 00:09:21.534
to honor my brother

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.000
in a way that I didn't before.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.434
I was just angry and upset
and I just turned that around

00:09:28.434 --> 00:09:31.133
to how can I give
the best of myself

00:09:31.133 --> 00:09:33.400
and give the best
of this character that,

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:35.734
you know, predominantly,
he chose to be his...

00:09:35.734 --> 00:09:38.400
You know,
he chose to be his sister.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:43.067
How can I do my best
in this film alongside my team,

00:09:43.067 --> 00:09:44.601
to be honorable to him,

00:09:44.601 --> 00:09:47.834
and that's how
we tackled it together.

00:09:47.834 --> 00:09:50.601
And I would say that's
the best way to pay an homage,

00:09:50.601 --> 00:09:52.300
working around it.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:54.234
The tribute paid
to Chadwick Boseman

00:09:54.234 --> 00:09:56.767
in the film is truly fantastic.
It's so great.

00:09:56.767 --> 00:09:58.834
Let's change
a little bit of mood.

00:09:58.834 --> 00:10:01.367
I want to talk about the music

00:10:01.367 --> 00:10:05.100
because these mixes
between the African rhythms

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:08.167
and these flutes
from the Mesoamerican cultures

00:10:08.167 --> 00:10:12.868
and the Mayan rhythms,
what can you tell me, Ryan,

00:10:12.868 --> 00:10:14.534
what do you want to do
with the music

00:10:14.534 --> 00:10:16.801
in Wakanda Forever?

00:10:16.801 --> 00:10:18.667
[Ryan] Yeah...

00:10:18.667 --> 00:10:21.234
We were able to work
with Ludwig Goransson,

00:10:21.234 --> 00:10:22.734
who was our composer
on the last film.

00:10:22.734 --> 00:10:25.968
I have a long working
relationship with him and...

00:10:26.968 --> 00:10:30.934
We try to work in a way
that's organic and holistic.

00:10:32.534 --> 00:10:33.868
We want to give audiences

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.300
a unique experience

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:41.067
and we also wanted
to make sure that,

00:10:41.067 --> 00:10:43.000
since we were exploring
another culture

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.200
and representing
another culture,

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:47.834
that we did the same
type of work that we did

00:10:47.834 --> 00:10:50.701
for the first film
in representing

00:10:50.701 --> 00:10:52.868
the cultures of Africa
in Wakanda.

00:10:52.868 --> 00:10:54.601
So Ludwig immediately

00:10:54.601 --> 00:10:56.067
read the first draft
of the script

00:10:56.067 --> 00:10:59.167
and booked time here
in Mexico City

00:10:59.167 --> 00:11:02.968
and also in the Yucatan region,
out of country,

00:11:02.968 --> 00:11:06.033
worked with musicians
that were from here

00:11:06.033 --> 00:11:07.767
in terms of making
contemporary songs,

00:11:07.767 --> 00:11:12.000
but also he worked
with musical archeologists,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.734
who kind of dedicated
their lives to the study

00:11:16.801 --> 00:11:19.601
of the types of music
that Mesoamericans

00:11:19.601 --> 00:11:21.901
would've made art with

00:11:21.901 --> 00:11:24.300
during the pre-Columbian times.

00:11:25.701 --> 00:11:28.734
And that part, I think,
was the most interesting

00:11:28.734 --> 00:11:32.267
because we found that a lot
of the motives of the film

00:11:32.267 --> 00:11:36.267
were present in the sonic scapes

00:11:36.267 --> 00:11:38.400
of the people from that time.
For instance,

00:11:39.601 --> 00:11:42.834
seashells, you know,
were a massive part

00:11:42.834 --> 00:11:45.133
of how they made music

00:11:45.133 --> 00:11:47.634
and it made a lot of sense
obviously because Talokán

00:11:47.634 --> 00:11:50.367
is now in the ocean in the MCU,

00:11:50.367 --> 00:11:52.601
so that really opened things up.
It would blew my mind

00:11:52.601 --> 00:11:54.467
how much they sounded
like French horns

00:11:54.467 --> 00:11:57.634
with the humanity that we had

00:11:57.634 --> 00:11:59.834
and the Fula flutes that we used
for Killmonger's theme

00:11:59.834 --> 00:12:01.901
-in the first film.
-Yeah.

00:12:01.901 --> 00:12:04.501
So it was fantastic. We were
also able to dig deeper

00:12:04.501 --> 00:12:06.501
into the music of the continent

00:12:06.501 --> 00:12:10.067
with both contemporary sound
and also use some

00:12:10.067 --> 00:12:11.934
of the more traditional music.

00:12:11.934 --> 00:12:14.067
I mean, it was
a fantastic opportunity

00:12:14.067 --> 00:12:15.400
and I think it's some
of Ludwig's best works

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:16.667
to be honest.

00:12:16.667 --> 00:12:19.200
Beautiful shells
that you find...

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.667
In the movie.

00:12:21.667 --> 00:12:24.400
Nakia and Shuri are...

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:26.767
beacons of inspiration.

00:12:26.767 --> 00:12:30.601
I think those characters
inspire a lot of people

00:12:30.601 --> 00:12:33.834
around the globe,
so first, how was

00:12:33.834 --> 00:12:35.834
experiencing

00:12:35.834 --> 00:12:38.868
that inspiration you provide

00:12:38.868 --> 00:12:41.400
with the characters and also,

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:44.767
in any sense, did that change
the way you approach

00:12:44.767 --> 00:12:46.801
the character
for this new movie?

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:51.400
It's very touching when
people relate to your character

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:54.868
and cite your character
as a source of inspiration,

00:12:54.868 --> 00:12:58.100
you know? In the first film,

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:01.968
we had so many female figures

00:13:01.968 --> 00:13:04.033
who were so different
from one another for...

00:13:06.767 --> 00:13:08.934
To be role models or people,
you know?

00:13:08.934 --> 00:13:13.400
There's a good population
of women in this story

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:18.601
that allow for young girls,
young people,

00:13:18.601 --> 00:13:21.000
to identify with one or another.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:25.100
Kids say "I relate to your
character Nakia for X reason"

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:28.601
or "I want to do STEM
thanks to Shuri"

00:13:28.601 --> 00:13:33.634
and it's lovely to be able
to be the vessel

00:13:33.634 --> 00:13:35.934
for that kind of...

00:13:35.934 --> 00:13:37.934
That kind of inspiration.

00:13:39.434 --> 00:13:43.200
And for this film, of course,
I think the death of Chadwick

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.968
really changed the direction
that we were going

00:13:46.968 --> 00:13:48.868
with this film

00:13:48.868 --> 00:13:53.968
and it became part of the
evolutions of the characters

00:13:53.968 --> 00:13:58.334
so, in this film, you know,
Nakia has matured,

00:13:58.334 --> 00:14:03.234
she's lost someone
near and dear to her.

00:14:03.234 --> 00:14:06.033
She's navigating her
grief in a special way,

00:14:06.033 --> 00:14:09.133
in a distinct way from,
say, how Shuri is doing it.

00:14:09.133 --> 00:14:11.901
And I think that having those
distinctions of how people

00:14:11.901 --> 00:14:13.767
deal with grief
in the first place

00:14:13.767 --> 00:14:15.701
is also extremely
helpful and healthy

00:14:16.667 --> 00:14:19.367
because grief doesn't look
the same on everyone.

00:14:19.367 --> 00:14:21.734
It's a very...

00:14:21.734 --> 00:14:24.667
It's a condition
with lots of colors

00:14:24.667 --> 00:14:25.968
and our film

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:31.968
showcases or explores grief

00:14:31.968 --> 00:14:35.968
in a number of different
and very helpful ways.

00:14:35.968 --> 00:14:37.567
All this, Letitia,
changed the way

00:14:37.567 --> 00:14:39.501
you approach the character?

00:14:39.501 --> 00:14:41.234
Yeah, for sure.

00:14:44.901 --> 00:14:48.334
In connection to what
Lupita just shared,

00:14:48.334 --> 00:14:49.567
I'm so beautifully

00:14:50.534 --> 00:14:53.000
seeing so many young girls

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.968
be empowered by these women

00:14:55.968 --> 00:14:58.901
and not forcing it,
you know what I mean?

00:14:58.901 --> 00:15:00.968
Like, we didn't do the film

00:15:00.968 --> 00:15:02.701
to get a particular result.

00:15:02.701 --> 00:15:04.434
We just wanted
to tell the truth.

00:15:04.434 --> 00:15:08.601
And I think when you do that,
people receive it, like, better?

00:15:08.601 --> 00:15:10.834
'Cause you're not trying
to force it down their throats,

00:15:10.834 --> 00:15:12.901
it's just like, we're just,
we're just existing

00:15:13.868 --> 00:15:18.601
in a world that is equal,
in a world that

00:15:18.601 --> 00:15:20.667
naturally you just feel
empowered when you look

00:15:20.667 --> 00:15:24.234
at these characters on screen
so that's the same for this one.

00:15:24.234 --> 00:15:28.501
And yeah, it is tricky
because we're looking at...

00:15:28.501 --> 00:15:30.067
We're looking
at the ways in which

00:15:31.334 --> 00:15:33.000
these beautiful women
from the first film

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:35.601
are impacted by someone
that they loved so much,

00:15:35.601 --> 00:15:38.534
so yes, it would cause you
to approach a character

00:15:38.534 --> 00:15:40.634
a little bit differently.

00:15:40.634 --> 00:15:42.868
But at the same time,
again, just staying on the path

00:15:42.868 --> 00:15:44.467
of telling the truth

00:15:44.467 --> 00:15:45.834
and that truth, again,

00:15:45.834 --> 00:15:48.767
can inspire and can also lead

00:15:48.767 --> 00:15:53.033
to many people feeling healed
as well from touching into...

00:15:53.033 --> 00:15:55.534
Touching into the ways
in which these characters

00:15:55.534 --> 00:15:58.100
are dealing with this situation.

00:15:59.501 --> 00:16:02.234
-Yes.
-Well, Mabel,

00:16:02.234 --> 00:16:06.100
what does it mean to you
to play this character,

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:07.901
which also has Mexican roots

00:16:07.901 --> 00:16:11.300
and presenting her
in Mexico City?

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:12.400
What does it mean?

00:16:13.634 --> 00:16:16.400
To see me. To see me
and embrace me

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:19.100
for the first time
and embrace my power.

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:21.334
I think there's
no better movie like this.

00:16:21.334 --> 00:16:23.968
There's no better movie
like Black Panther

00:16:23.968 --> 00:16:26.834
to introduce a world
like what Talokán is.

00:16:26.834 --> 00:16:30.234
To introduce a diversity
of faces, of colors,

00:16:30.234 --> 00:16:32.133
of shapes, you know?

00:16:32.133 --> 00:16:34.267
I think it's amazing

00:16:34.267 --> 00:16:37.267
that a movie like this can start

00:16:37.267 --> 00:16:41.400
to show beautiful faces,
beautiful women

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:47.901
and powerful men around Mexico,
Latin America and the world.

00:16:47.901 --> 00:16:51.701
And embracing our cultures
and our origins,

00:16:51.701 --> 00:16:53.267
you know? I mean,

00:16:53.267 --> 00:16:54.534
we're talking about

00:16:54.534 --> 00:16:57.300
the fact that maybe
we're different stories,

00:16:57.300 --> 00:16:59.434
but we have the same movement,

00:16:59.434 --> 00:17:02.200
we are moved by the same needs.

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:06.801
I have the need for many
girls and boys to feel that,

00:17:06.801 --> 00:17:10.400
even if I didn't have
the opportunity before to dream

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:13.133
that I could be part
of a film like this,

00:17:13.133 --> 00:17:16.067
I don't want children today
to question that

00:17:16.067 --> 00:17:18.033
they can dream about it.

00:17:18.033 --> 00:17:21.934
I want adults to be able
to see their stories,

00:17:21.934 --> 00:17:24.801
their languages, their origins,
their music represented.

00:17:24.801 --> 00:17:27.367
And I think to be part
of a movie like this,

00:17:28.701 --> 00:17:31.801
to bring Talokán,
with Tenoch, with Alex,

00:17:31.801 --> 00:17:35.234
with all this universe that
was part of what changed the...

00:17:35.234 --> 00:17:37.434
That legacy of Black Panther

00:17:37.434 --> 00:17:41.400
can give us the hope
of representing

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.534
a diversity
of stories in our cinema

00:17:44.534 --> 00:17:47.367
around the world,
not only in Mexico.

00:17:47.367 --> 00:17:49.934
And I agree with you.
And I gotta say,

00:17:49.934 --> 00:17:52.934
Ryan and Nate, as Mexican,

00:17:52.934 --> 00:17:55.367
to see

00:17:55.367 --> 00:17:59.167
those references in the movie,
so well respected,

00:18:00.734 --> 00:18:03.667
it's fantastic
and I'm grateful for that.

00:18:03.667 --> 00:18:05.868
But how hard was the process

00:18:05.868 --> 00:18:07.367
to come to that conclusion,

00:18:07.367 --> 00:18:09.234
of what are we
going to be seeing

00:18:09.234 --> 00:18:13.868
in this submerged city,
in this beautiful Talokán?

00:18:13.868 --> 00:18:15.868
I mean, I think once Ryan
had the idea

00:18:15.868 --> 00:18:17.901
to sort of anchor
the world of Talokán

00:18:17.901 --> 00:18:20.367
in Mesoamerican culture
and really be inspired

00:18:20.367 --> 00:18:22.267
both by ancient
Mayan civilization

00:18:22.267 --> 00:18:24.434
and present day
Mayan civilization,

00:18:24.434 --> 00:18:27.501
then it's just the process
of finding the right consultants

00:18:27.501 --> 00:18:29.934
to help us try to get it
as right as we can,

00:18:29.934 --> 00:18:31.901
you know? I don't think
any of us pretend

00:18:31.901 --> 00:18:34.234
to be historians,
but you do want,

00:18:34.234 --> 00:18:35.767
if you're going to pay
homage to a place,

00:18:35.767 --> 00:18:38.167
you do want to get it right
so we worked with,

00:18:38.167 --> 00:18:39.701
you know, Dr. Aldana,

00:18:39.701 --> 00:18:41.868
who's an academic
out of the US who spent

00:18:41.868 --> 00:18:43.734
a lot of time in the region.

00:18:43.734 --> 00:18:47.334
We had an on-set interpreter
for Yucatec Mayan dialect

00:18:47.334 --> 00:18:49.667
so that Mabel, Tenoch, and Alex

00:18:49.667 --> 00:18:51.534
always had a touchstone
of someone

00:18:51.534 --> 00:18:53.567
who is from the region
who could speak it correctly,

00:18:54.767 --> 00:18:56.434
but I think trying
to get those details

00:18:56.434 --> 00:18:58.267
as right as possible

00:18:58.267 --> 00:19:00.834
hopefully will build
a world that people

00:19:00.834 --> 00:19:04.200
feel is as cared for
as Wakanda is

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:05.300
because it's important
when you're,

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:06.601
if you're seeing Talokán,

00:19:06.601 --> 00:19:08.067
as referenced earlier,

00:19:08.067 --> 00:19:11.367
you don't want it to feel
like we didn't take the time

00:19:11.367 --> 00:19:13.167
because really is a love letter

00:19:13.167 --> 00:19:15.334
to both of these worlds.

00:19:15.334 --> 00:19:18.067
And you brought back Ruth Carter

00:19:18.067 --> 00:19:20.601
in that same order of ideas,

00:19:20.601 --> 00:19:22.033
so I...

00:19:22.033 --> 00:19:24.434
I don't know. I assume
she was very important

00:19:24.434 --> 00:19:26.567
to closure everything.

00:19:26.567 --> 00:19:29.067
Absolutely. Absolutely.

00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:32.501
Ruth was back,
Hannah Beachler was back,

00:19:33.767 --> 00:19:35.901
we worked with a great
visual development team

00:19:35.901 --> 00:19:39.601
that works at Marvel Studios
supervised by Ryan,

00:19:39.601 --> 00:19:40.801
and I feel...

00:19:40.801 --> 00:19:42.200
[murmuring]

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:44.033
Yeah. Like...

00:19:44.033 --> 00:19:46.834
So we had, we had all these

00:19:46.834 --> 00:19:48.534
incredible professionals

00:19:48.534 --> 00:19:50.501
who we had relationships with.
We knew each other's

00:19:51.934 --> 00:19:53.634
dispositions
when it came to work

00:19:53.634 --> 00:19:56.968
so it was actually
fantastic to get back to it.

00:19:56.968 --> 00:19:58.400
And I gotta say,

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:01.167
you know, yesterday I was able
to go to the museum

00:20:01.167 --> 00:20:03.400
that's like right
across from our hotel,

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:06.634
and we went to the Mayan
exhibit with my family

00:20:06.634 --> 00:20:10.067
and it was actually
astonishing to see

00:20:10.067 --> 00:20:14.100
all the things that Dr. Aldana
had shown us, like, pictures,

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:16.000
some of the things
we kind of rebuilt.

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:19.067
To see the actual things,
it was like...

00:20:19.067 --> 00:20:21.133
It was the most, maybe one
of the most surreal experiences

00:20:21.133 --> 00:20:23.100
I've ever had as a filmmaker,
you know? 'Cause

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:24.901
we made this film
during the pandemic.

00:20:24.901 --> 00:20:27.501
It was not easy
to travel to the places

00:20:27.501 --> 00:20:29.467
the same way we did
on the first film.

00:20:29.467 --> 00:20:32.200
So we were mostly traveling
digitally, you know what I mean?

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:36.100
Looking at Google Earth,
and looking at photographs.

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:37.834
We were 3D printing things.

00:20:37.834 --> 00:20:40.200
And it was, to walk in there

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:43.267
and see the actual artifacts,

00:20:43.267 --> 00:20:45.100
you know, from what
we studied, to see

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:48.934
the jade workings
and the headdresses

00:20:48.934 --> 00:20:51.601
and all of the texts, you know?

00:20:51.601 --> 00:20:55.434
To actually see
it was deeply moving.

00:20:55.434 --> 00:20:56.801
It was deeply moving.

00:20:56.801 --> 00:21:00.767
I felt like
a spiritual connection,

00:21:00.767 --> 00:21:03.501
like, I felt like we did
the right thing, you know?

00:21:03.501 --> 00:21:05.501
We made the right choices,
you never know.

00:21:07.467 --> 00:21:08.834
I think that, for me,

00:21:10.067 --> 00:21:13.534
the idea of consulting work,
like, the industry, you know?

00:21:13.534 --> 00:21:15.934
The industry of saying
I'm not letting the fact

00:21:15.934 --> 00:21:17.901
that you don't know
be an excuse not to do it,

00:21:17.901 --> 00:21:19.734
you know what I mean? Like,
going to find the people

00:21:19.734 --> 00:21:22.000
who know, paying them,
you know what I mean?

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:25.067
And letting them inform
what was happening.

00:21:25.067 --> 00:21:30.033
I hope that we can be examples
of a new type of filmmaking

00:21:30.033 --> 00:21:32.133
-at this level we're making.
-[Letitia] Love that.

00:21:32.133 --> 00:21:35.367
Before we go
to the fans' questions,

00:21:35.367 --> 00:21:39.133
and regarding the fans
and everything,

00:21:39.133 --> 00:21:42.467
what are you most excited about
for the fans

00:21:42.467 --> 00:21:44.734
to see in Wakanda Forever?

00:21:44.734 --> 00:21:45.968
-Me?
-Yeah.

00:21:45.968 --> 00:21:47.400
-Wow.
-That's a...

00:21:48.434 --> 00:21:49.767
You're the man!

00:21:49.767 --> 00:21:51.000
I mean, there's several of us,

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:52.133
you know what I mean?

00:21:52.133 --> 00:21:53.834
No, it's you. It's you.

00:21:56.467 --> 00:21:57.801
What I would say...

00:21:57.801 --> 00:21:59.400
What I would say...

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:01.767
It's hard for me to pick
one thing, you know?

00:22:01.767 --> 00:22:02.801
A lot went into this.

00:22:04.434 --> 00:22:06.000
A lot was sacrificed
by everyone,

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:07.200
individually, collectively.

00:22:08.934 --> 00:22:11.934
I've never been as proud
of a movie as this

00:22:11.934 --> 00:22:14.968
and I've been working
for over a third of my life,

00:22:14.968 --> 00:22:16.601
you know what I'm saying?
In this medium.

00:22:18.767 --> 00:22:21.601
When I watch the film,
I feel the love that we have

00:22:21.601 --> 00:22:24.901
for each other, the love
that we have for the medium,

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:28.501
the love that we have
for our dearly departed friend.

00:22:29.801 --> 00:22:31.234
And I also feel

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:35.334
the wish to connect with people,
you know what I mean?

00:22:35.334 --> 00:22:37.300
I feel it. Like,

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:38.667
as we were showing
the film to people,

00:22:39.934 --> 00:22:42.834
I think that part works,
like, people would say "Hey,

00:22:42.834 --> 00:22:46.434
this film reminds me of when
I lost this person that I love"

00:22:46.434 --> 00:22:48.968
or "This film reminds
me when I was going through

00:22:48.968 --> 00:22:52.501
this emotion or when I was
coming of age in this way."

00:22:53.567 --> 00:22:56.434
So I'm actually looking
forward to audiences

00:22:56.434 --> 00:22:57.767
just having a good time
at the movies,

00:22:57.767 --> 00:22:58.868
but also connecting to what

00:22:58.868 --> 00:23:00.901
we were feeling at the time.

00:23:00.901 --> 00:23:03.434
And now let's move on
to the fan questions.

00:23:03.434 --> 00:23:05.868
And the first one
is for you, Mabel.

00:23:05.868 --> 00:23:08.300
It says, "how would you
describe the relationship

00:23:08.300 --> 00:23:10.334
between Namora and Namor?"

00:23:13.334 --> 00:23:15.567
For me it's such a beautiful
relationship because,

00:23:15.567 --> 00:23:17.901
all of a sudden,
I feel Namora so visceral

00:23:17.901 --> 00:23:21.400
and so ready
to protect her people,

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:26.434
to go to war to give her life
for Namor and all this,

00:23:26.434 --> 00:23:30.434
that when she has to calm down

00:23:30.434 --> 00:23:31.667
and listen to him,

00:23:31.667 --> 00:23:34.634
then you mix two generations,
you know?

00:23:34.634 --> 00:23:37.567
The wisdom of their God,

00:23:37.567 --> 00:23:42.434
of the person who has passed on
to her this protective thing,

00:23:42.434 --> 00:23:44.467
loyalty, love for her people,

00:23:44.467 --> 00:23:47.234
for her nation, for her culture,

00:23:47.234 --> 00:23:52.868
but who at the same time
passes on to her this wisdom

00:23:52.868 --> 00:23:55.400
that only someone like him
can have

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:58.000
and who passes on to her
another kind of knowledge.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:00.801
So, mixing
these two relationships,

00:24:00.801 --> 00:24:03.200
these two ways of seeing life,

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:05.567
seems to me one
of the most beautiful things.

00:24:05.567 --> 00:24:09.834
I tried to work with Tenoch
to build our relationship

00:24:09.834 --> 00:24:12.501
because we said
"Let's put a little bit

00:24:12.501 --> 00:24:15.767
of our Mexican culture.
We Mexicans are family,

00:24:15.767 --> 00:24:20.300
we are warm,
we are protective, loyal."

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:23.167
So, we were trying
to have gestures

00:24:23.167 --> 00:24:25.901
of being a family,
of being warriors,

00:24:25.901 --> 00:24:27.567
of taking care of each other,

00:24:27.567 --> 00:24:30.267
of standing up for our people,
for our nation.

00:24:31.367 --> 00:24:33.467
That's so nice!
The next question

00:24:33.467 --> 00:24:35.234
is for you, Letitia.

00:24:35.234 --> 00:24:38.267
They say "Shuri is such
an incredible character.

00:24:38.267 --> 00:24:41.334
Fans would like to know
what can they expect from her

00:24:41.334 --> 00:24:42.968
in Wakanda Forever?"

00:24:42.968 --> 00:24:45.601
We can expect a young woman

00:24:45.601 --> 00:24:48.000
going through the journey
of womanhood.

00:24:49.167 --> 00:24:52.434
We can expect to see
Shuri in ways

00:24:52.434 --> 00:24:54.501
that we never thought
we would have before.

00:24:55.701 --> 00:24:58.400
There's a maturity to her
that's really lovely.

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:00.601
It can speak
to a lot of young people

00:25:00.601 --> 00:25:03.200
who are probably
on that same path as well.

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:06.601
And we can expect
a lot of her lively,

00:25:06.601 --> 00:25:10.133
bubbly self, but just it's...

00:25:10.133 --> 00:25:12.033
There's...

00:25:12.033 --> 00:25:14.400
Real things
that she's dealing with

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:16.434
in comparison to the first film

00:25:16.434 --> 00:25:19.267
and we see the ways
in which she handles that

00:25:19.267 --> 00:25:21.133
and hopefully it can be
of inspiration

00:25:21.133 --> 00:25:22.734
and healing to others.

00:25:24.033 --> 00:25:27.267
Can expect a lot more still,
but you gotta see it.

00:25:27.267 --> 00:25:29.434
You gotta see it when
it comes out this weekend.

00:25:29.434 --> 00:25:31.067
And I'm going to allow
myself to do this.

00:25:31.067 --> 00:25:32.834
Lupita, this question is for you

00:25:32.834 --> 00:25:34.334
and they told me
to ask it in Spanish.

00:25:34.334 --> 00:25:35.734
Okay.

00:25:35.734 --> 00:25:38.734
First of all,
they say they love you.

00:25:38.734 --> 00:25:42.100
Heart emoji and everything,
very nice.

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:44.734
And then they ask "how did you
react when you found out

00:25:44.734 --> 00:25:48.868
that Black Panther 2 was going
to highlight Mexican culture,

00:25:48.868 --> 00:25:51.334
that you were going to speak
a little bit of Spanish

00:25:51.334 --> 00:25:52.601
-in the movie?"
-Yes.

00:25:54.133 --> 00:25:55.200
It surprised me,

00:25:57.467 --> 00:25:58.501
but I also...

00:26:02.167 --> 00:26:04.467
Felt very grateful?

00:26:04.467 --> 00:26:07.467
Is that how you say it?
Yeah, thankful to Ryan

00:26:07.467 --> 00:26:10.667
for doing that
because for a long time, I...

00:26:10.667 --> 00:26:14.300
I've wanted to speak
Spanish in a movie

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:17.300
and I never imagined

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:20.501
it was going to happen
with Black Panther.

00:26:20.501 --> 00:26:24.434
So I read the script
and I saw the Spanish words

00:26:24.434 --> 00:26:27.133
and I felt very...

00:26:27.133 --> 00:26:29.701
Very... How do you say it?

00:26:29.701 --> 00:26:32.267
-Pride.
-Yes! Of course.

00:26:32.267 --> 00:26:35.300
I was proud of doing it.

00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:37.968
Well, that's fantastic.
We thank you,

00:26:37.968 --> 00:26:40.767
our dear adopted Mexican.

00:26:40.767 --> 00:26:42.901
Of course.

00:26:42.901 --> 00:26:47.868
And Ryan, I want to ask you
about this part,

00:26:47.868 --> 00:26:50.567
of bringing the Spanish

00:26:50.567 --> 00:26:52.334
and the Mayan world,

00:26:53.434 --> 00:26:56.534
why was that
very important for you?

00:26:56.534 --> 00:26:58.434
Why was it important?

00:26:58.434 --> 00:26:59.467
Yeah. Language

00:27:00.634 --> 00:27:04.934
is a big motif
in these films,

00:27:04.934 --> 00:27:06.000
this series of films.

00:27:07.133 --> 00:27:11.734
This idea of how
the active conquest,

00:27:11.734 --> 00:27:14.334
historical conquest
or colonization can affect

00:27:14.334 --> 00:27:16.701
the way that people speak.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:19.868
It was an idea that
actually Nate and...

00:27:21.901 --> 00:27:23.734
Chad I think
discussed in Civil War,

00:27:23.734 --> 00:27:27.267
even before
I was hired, you know?

00:27:27.267 --> 00:27:30.534
And I can't express
the joy I felt

00:27:30.534 --> 00:27:32.067
when I watched Civil War
for the first time.

00:27:32.067 --> 00:27:34.300
I saw a very early cut.
We were trying to figure out

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:36.801
if we were going to work
together, and my mind was blown

00:27:38.834 --> 00:27:40.467
when T'Challa and T'Chaka
spoke to each other

00:27:40.467 --> 00:27:42.300
at the UN in a language,
it was a language

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:45.767
that I had never heard before,
a South African dialect.

00:27:45.767 --> 00:27:48.067
And I immediately thought "Wow,

00:27:48.067 --> 00:27:50.234
they invented
a language for the movie."

00:27:50.234 --> 00:27:52.000
You know what I'm saying?
"This sounds awesome."

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:54.434
And I talked to Nate,
like a few minutes

00:27:54.434 --> 00:27:55.734
after I watched the movie,
and he was like "No,

00:27:55.734 --> 00:27:57.434
that's actually the language
that the actor

00:27:57.434 --> 00:27:59.067
that we casted spoke."

00:27:59.067 --> 00:28:00.534
And I was like "First of all,
that was one of the most

00:28:00.534 --> 00:28:02.200
beautiful languages
I've ever heard.

00:28:03.267 --> 00:28:05.467
Second of all, this is awesome."

00:28:05.467 --> 00:28:07.234
Because the films that
I loved when I was young

00:28:07.234 --> 00:28:09.901
often gave an additional
respect to languages,

00:28:09.901 --> 00:28:11.367
even when it didn't have to.

00:28:11.367 --> 00:28:13.200
I think when watching
the Tolkien adaptations

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:15.467
that Peter Jackson did,
when the elves speak

00:28:15.467 --> 00:28:19.100
their own language
and you feel like the geo...

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:22.133
I think geopolitical film
is the best way

00:28:22.133 --> 00:28:23.868
you understand
why people communicate

00:28:23.868 --> 00:28:24.968
and why they're entirely
different.

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:29.701
And especially these films,
we kind of let colonization

00:28:29.701 --> 00:28:31.133
be a thing that we wrestle with,

00:28:31.133 --> 00:28:32.434
it became
all the more important.

00:28:33.601 --> 00:28:36.434
It's very important in America

00:28:36.434 --> 00:28:37.834
as it is on the continent,
you know?

00:28:37.834 --> 00:28:38.968
You can learn a lot

00:28:38.968 --> 00:28:40.601
about people by how they speak.

00:28:40.601 --> 00:28:43.634
As we learn more about Mayan
culture and learn that...

00:28:47.367 --> 00:28:49.868
A large Mayan population
is still in this place

00:28:49.868 --> 00:28:51.200
and they
still speak the language.

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:53.367
It's something
they hold on to with pride.

00:28:53.367 --> 00:28:56.501
It made a lot of sense to make
sure that the Talokánians

00:28:56.501 --> 00:28:58.267
held on to that as well,

00:28:58.267 --> 00:29:02.067
especially due
to Namor's character.

00:29:02.067 --> 00:29:03.834
It felt like he was
a person that was trying

00:29:03.834 --> 00:29:06.067
to hold onto
everything that he could.

00:29:09.267 --> 00:29:11.000
So we took it seriously.

00:29:12.267 --> 00:29:14.167
It's like seven languages
spoken in the movie.

00:29:14.167 --> 00:29:15.200
-[Ryan] Yeah.
-[Lupita] Wow.

00:29:16.834 --> 00:29:18.534
We had a lot to follow
with that.

00:29:18.534 --> 00:29:21.801
I'm a monolingual director,
you know what I'm saying?

00:29:21.801 --> 00:29:24.434
So it was not
without complications.

00:29:25.601 --> 00:29:28.667
Not without unintentional
humor at times.

00:29:30.234 --> 00:29:34.801
We have Josue, who's,
you know, many times

00:29:34.801 --> 00:29:37.501
the only person on set
who spoke fluent Mayan.

00:29:37.501 --> 00:29:39.133
Man, he was working hard.
You know what I mean?

00:29:39.133 --> 00:29:41.133
We would bring him in
to watch cuts of the movie

00:29:41.133 --> 00:29:42.734
and he was like "No,
you gotta fix this."

00:29:43.834 --> 00:29:44.968
-Yeah.
-"What they're saying

00:29:44.968 --> 00:29:46.300
is not what you want them
to say."

00:29:47.868 --> 00:29:52.300
But he had a deep,
I think, as all Mayans do,

00:29:52.300 --> 00:29:54.834
as he was explaining to me,
he had a very deep, deep,

00:29:54.834 --> 00:29:58.067
deep sense of duty

00:29:58.067 --> 00:30:00.701
to make sure we got this right.
And he would not...

00:30:03.601 --> 00:30:07.534
Hesitate to stress that upon us
and to stress upon the actors.

00:30:07.534 --> 00:30:09.868
"It's very important
that you do this the right way."

00:30:12.300 --> 00:30:13.334
I was honored.

00:30:14.801 --> 00:30:17.701
I feel a deep sense of gratitude
because I cannot imagine

00:30:18.834 --> 00:30:20.701
being the only person
on the set having to represent

00:30:20.701 --> 00:30:24.734
my whole culture amongst people
who are not as familiar with it

00:30:24.734 --> 00:30:29.400
as necessary and I hope
we did those languages

00:30:29.400 --> 00:30:31.367
and those cultures justice,
you know what I'm saying?

00:30:31.367 --> 00:30:32.934
Well, the truth is that
you have to be very,

00:30:32.934 --> 00:30:35.601
very excited about
this movie, everyone.

00:30:35.601 --> 00:30:37.334
If you weren't already
hyped up enough,

00:30:37.334 --> 00:30:40.434
I think now they're going to be
even more excited

00:30:40.434 --> 00:30:43.701
to see this movie and check
all of your incredible,

00:30:43.701 --> 00:30:45.901
incredible work
in Wakanda Forever.

00:30:45.901 --> 00:30:47.534
Thank you all so much.

00:30:47.534 --> 00:30:50.033
I hope you enjoyed
your time in Mexico City.

00:30:50.033 --> 00:30:54.200
Please, Lupita, eat tacos,
pozole, and quesadillas.

00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:56.467
You gotta try everything.
Thank you very much

00:30:56.467 --> 00:30:58.234
for being here with us.

00:30:58.234 --> 00:30:59.667
And thank you all for watching!

00:30:59.667 --> 00:31:02.067
We can't wait for everyone
to experience the movie.

00:31:02.067 --> 00:31:04.400
Get your tickets now
for Marvel Studios'

00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:08.467
Black Panther: Wakanda Forever
in theaters everywhere.

00:31:08.467 --> 00:31:10.534
-Bye, bye. Thank you.
-Thank you.

00:31:10.534 --> 00:31:11.701
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

